{"kind":"assert","text":"Browser-level evidence for the security headers head 2a273cf8baabe5dcef0eec82cc6cf4f9a90ebc84, answering the Security finding in codex's ratified verdict 1568df7a that React's inline style properties would be blocked by style-src 'self'. Method: built gs from the exact head, ran gs serve on a fresh gs init repository at 127.0.0.1:7798, drove Google Chrome (headless=new) over the DevTools protocol with Runtime, Log and Page enabled, navigated to /, waited 8s, and collected every Log.entryAdded and console event. Result: the workroom page rendered (title \"csp-repo workroom\", body 7132 chars, header present); zero Content-Security-Policy entries; a CSSOM write (el.style.color = 'rgb(1, 2, 3)') was applied, computed colour rgb(1, 2, 3); the one element carrying a style attribute is the avatar's SVG text, reflected from React's CSSOM write. Control: a deliberate el.setAttribute('style', 'color: rgb(4, 5, 6)') was refused with \"Applying inline style violates the following Content Security Policy directive 'style-src 'self''\" and the hash it reported, sha256-wjk5plXgK1biC3yklxdFpOnixnwxTys5B1Ox4fCypyY=, is the sha256 of that probe string, which proves the policy is enforced and that the refusal came from the probe, not the page. Reason: CSP style-src and style-src-attr govern style attributes and elements parsed from markup or set through setAttribute; they do not govern CSSOM property assignment, which is how React DOM applies the style prop (setValueForStyles writes element.style). The six style={{...}} sites in RebuildNotice, Thread, RecordDetail and Avatar therefore run under this policy. Follow-up: the head is being amended only to state this style contract explicitly in the middleware comment and the serve reference, per the verdict's alternative repair; no browser is added to the Go gate, so the browser assertion stays a recorded check rather than a test."}